.TH @BIN@ 1 "@DATE@" "pwdx @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@BIN@ \- stateless, offline password derivation
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @BIN@ key new
.br
.B @BIN@ key import\-ssh
.I FILE
.br
.B @BIN@ key id
.br
.B @BIN@ get
.I SITE
.B \-\-user
.I USER
.RB [ \-\-counter\ \fIN\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-profile\ \fIPROFILE\fP ]
.RB [ \-\-length\ \fIL\fP ]
.br
.B @BIN@ selftest
.SH DESCRIPTION
Turns one memorable password into a different, long, random\-looking password for every site. The same inputs always give
the same output on every device. Nothing is stored or sent anywhere. A secret 256\-bit key, kept in an encrypted key file,
is mixed into every derivation (Argon2id, HMAC\-SHA512, HKDF).
.PP
Secrets are read only from a hidden terminal prompt, never from arguments or environment variables.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B key new
Create a new random key and save it encrypted under a key\-file passphrase.
.TP
.BI "key import\-ssh " FILE
Import the seed of an OpenSSH Ed25519 private key (plain or passphrase\-protected).
.TP
.B key id
Print the key's non\-secret 8\-character Key ID.
.TP
.BI "get " SITE
Derive and print the password for SITE.
.TP
.B selftest
Run a built\-in known\-answer check.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-user " USER"
Username or email for the site (required).
.TP
.BI \-\-counter " N"
Rotation counter, starting at 1.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " PROFILE"
One of full (default), alnum, passphrase, pin.
.TP
.BI \-\-length " L"
Length in characters (words for passphrase).
.TP
.BI \-\-key " PATH"
Key file to use.
.TP
.B \-\-stdin
Read secrets from standard input (for automation and tests).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pwdx/key.pwdxkey
Default encrypted key file (also your backup). Overridden by \fB\-\-key\fP or $PWDX_KEY_FILE.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
@BIN@ key new
@BIN@ get github.com \-\-user me@example.com
.fi
.SH NOTES
Changing the memorable password or the key changes every derived password. Without a backup of the key file, derived
passwords cannot be recovered.
